#
# Security tier: default
#
# What a plain build ships, and on by default: build.sh composes it into
# every image built without profiles on its line. Every tool and builtin is
# built, and each behaves 1:1 with bash, dash and GNU for anything a script
# does. STRICT=1 is the only lean: a divergence only where benign input is
# unaffected and hostile input was dangerous (escaping a remote peer's utmp
# host field, refusing a symlinked component under mkdir -p).
#
# The four tiers, each a whole answer -- name one last, after the rest of the
# default line, in place of this one:
#
#   sh build.sh debug_none limbo desktop wifi serial sec_hardened
#
#   sec_reference   the reference exactly, holes and all (STRICT=0)
#   sec_default     this
#   sec_hardened    refuse where default sanitises (STRICT=2)
#   sec_locked      a kiosk or appliance: a short allow list of tools
#
# Only the default promises the reference's behaviour. The others are a
# choice, and what they refuse is refused on purpose.
#

#
# Yama. Floodlight's restricted launches keep a confined child from being
# attached to by its siblings, which needs ptrace_scope 1 or above -- the
# default Yama boots with. The LSM list the kernel defaults to already
# names yama. INTEGRITY is on by default under SECURITY and brings in
# nothing any profile asks for, so it stays out.
#
CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y
CONFIG_INTEGRITY=n

CONFIG_MOONWATER_STRICT=1
